### 1. The Root Cause: Per-Character Interop & PDFKit Calls on iOS
In the iOS implementation, the [extractTextSpatially](file:///Users/sunil/Downloads/PDFParser/shared/src/iosMain/kotlin/com/ssbmax/pdfparser/parser/SpatialTextExtractor.kt#L21-L50) function loops through every single character index of the page:

```kotlin
for (i in 0 until pageString.length) {
    val char = pageString[i]
    if (char.isWhitespace()) continue

    val selection = page.selectionForRange(platform.Foundation.NSMakeRange(i.toULong(), 1UL)) ?: continue
    val rectVal = selection.boundsForPage(page)
    ...
}
```

For every non-whitespace character in the PDF page (which can easily be 2,000–5,000 characters):
* **High-Level Layout Calculations:** It calls `selectionForRange` and `boundsForPage` on `PDFPage` and `PDFSelection`. These are high-level PDFKit layout and rendering engine search calls. They are not designed to be invoked thousands of times in a tight loop.
* **Kotlin/Native Bridge Overhead:** In Kotlin Multiplatform (KMP) for iOS, every call to an Objective-C/Cocoa framework (like PDFKit or Foundation) has to cross the Objective-C runtime boundary. Doing this twice per character (`selectionForRange` + `boundsForPage`) for thousands of characters incurs massive **CPU interop overhead** (type wrapping, memory management checks, pointer conversions).

